from pathlib import Path
p=Path('items/thm-nrqmx-finite-metaplectic-construction.md');s=p.read_text()
a=s.index('[F4]');b=s.index('[F6]')
s=s[:a]+r'''[F4] The local reconstruction in the Proof of [[lem-nrqmx-finite-maslov-phase-parity]], steps 1.1–1.3, constructs the unique normalized propagator for every piecewise-smooth real homogeneous quadratic Hamiltonian. It is a unitary Schwartz automorphism, depends continuously on time together with its inverse, and has the Weyl covariance of its classical matrix solution. Near the identity a smooth local lift is $L(S)=C_{CA^{-1}}E_AT_{A^{-1}B}$, with the chirp, coordinate and Fourier-multiplier factors explicitly defined there. Their parameter derivatives are homogeneous symmetrically ordered quadratics, and their Schwartz seminorm bounds are locally uniform. This is a proved local construction, not an external time-slicing theorem.

[F5] The same supplier proves the sign ambiguity for normalized quadratic propagators with the same endpoint. Its Proof, steps 2.1–2.3, obtains $Z=(C+iD)(A+iB)^{-1}$ and $c^2\det(A+iB)=1$ for the transported vacuum; step 3.1 combines this with phase uniqueness to give $U_2=\pm U_1$. In particular its stated generating-chart conclusion is available, and the local calculation actually applies at all endpoints. [[lem-nrqmx-finite-maslov-phase-parity]]

'''+s[b:]
a=s.index('1.2 Define');b=s.index('2.1 Every')
s=s[:a]+r'''1.2 Define $\operatorname{Mp}(2r,\mathbb R)$ as the set of endpoints of the normalized evolutions in [F4] along piecewise-smooth coefficient paths $K(t)=K(t)^T$, with $H_t=\tfrac14\sum_{j,k}K_{jk}(t)(R_jR_k+R_kR_j)$ and no linear or scalar term. The operator acts on the common Schwartz space. Concatenation gives products: first evolve by $U_2$, then evolve by $U_1$ with its initial vector $U_2f$, obtaining $U_1U_2$. The reversed evolution $U(T-t)U(T)^{-1}$ starts at $I$, ends at $U(T)^{-1}$, and is generated by $-H_{T-t}$, so inverses also belong to the set. By [F4], each endpoint implements the symplectic solution $\dot S=J_0K(t)S$. If two matrices had the same implementing unitary, differentiating their Weyl covariances on Schwartz space would give $(S-S')R=0$. Applied to the vacuum, $Q_kg_0=\sqrt{\hbar/2}g_{e_k}$ and $P_kg_0=i\sqrt{\hbar/2}g_{e_k}$ by [F6]; the reality of the rows forces both their position and momentum coefficients to vanish. Thus the endpoint is unique. For two lifts, $\pi(M_1M_2)=\pi(M_1)\pi(M_2)$ by conjugating the quadratures, so $\pi$ is a homomorphism. [F1, F4, F6, given, algebra]

'''+s[b:]
a=s.index('3.1 To bound');b=s.index('4.1 The identity')
s=s[:a]+r'''3.1 If two normalized lifts lie over the same grouped matrix $S$, the Gaussian determinant calculation in [F5] makes them differ by sign. This already holds for every $S$: the matrix $A+iB$ used there is invertible by symplecticity, including when either real block is singular. In particular it supplies the required invertible-$B$ chart argument without an imported Maslov-index parity formula. Thus every fiber has at most two elements. [F4, F5, step 1.2, algebra]

'''+s[b:]
a=s.index('5.1 It remains')
s=s[:a]+r'''5.1 Fix $S_0$ and a normalized lift $M_0$. On a matrix neighborhood $\mathcal B$ of $S_0$ small enough that $SS_0^{-1}$ lies in the local neighborhood of [F4], put $\sigma(S)=L(SS_0^{-1})M_0$. The local factors are endpoints of homogeneous quadratic paths starting at the identity, as proved in [F4], so $\sigma(S)$ belongs to $\operatorname{Mp}$ and has endpoint $S$. It depends continuously on $S$, as does its inverse, pointwise in the Schwartz topology: the explicit chirp and linear-coordinate factors have uniformly bounded seminorm estimates on compact parameter neighborhoods, and the Fourier factors are fixed continuous maps. These estimates also justify continuity when composing such a varying local factor with a varying Schwartz vector. Step 4.1 shows every lift over $S$ is exactly $\sigma(S)$ or $-\sigma(S)$. [F4, step 1.2, step 2.1, step 4.1, construct, algebra]

5.2 The endpoint map is continuous for the specified operator topology, without assuming joint continuity of arbitrary Schwartz-operator composition. For each row $j$ and mode $k$, covariance and [F6] give $\langle R_jMg_0,Mg_{e_k}\rangle=\sqrt{\hbar/2}(S_{j,Q_k}+iS_{j,P_k})$, where the pairing is linear in its first argument. On the left $R_j$ is continuous on Schwartz space, its inclusion into $L^2$ is continuous, and only the two fixed input vectors $g_0,g_{e_k}$ are used. Thus every real matrix entry of $\pi(M)$ is continuous. Over $\mathcal B$, the scalar $\varepsilon(M):=\langle Mg_0,\sigma(\pi(M))g_0\rangle$ is continuous, takes exactly the values $+1,-1$, and distinguishes the two lifts since $\|g_0\|=1$. Therefore the two inverse images $\varepsilon^{-1}(1)$ and $\varepsilon^{-1}(-1)$ in $\pi^{-1}(\mathcal B)$ are disjoint open sheets; on each, $\pi$ and $\pm\sigma$ are continuous inverse maps onto $\mathcal B$. This proves the two-fold covering in the given topology. [F4, F6, step 1.1, step 4.1, step 5.1, algebra]

6.1 The same charts make the set a topological group. Locally its elements are $\varepsilon\sigma(S)$. Products and inverses of their explicit finite-parameter factors depend continuously on the matrix parameters and on each Schwartz vector, by the uniform seminorm bounds in step 5.1; their endpoint matrices multiply and invert continuously. The sign relative to a local section at the resulting endpoint is recovered by the continuous vacuum pairing in step 5.2. Hence multiplication and inversion are continuous in these charts, which have precisely the operator topology by step 5.2. Finally every defining normalized path is continuous in that topology by [F4], including its inverse path, so it joins its endpoint to $I$. Consequently the group is path connected. Together with step 4.1 this proves the connected double cover with kernel $\{I,-I\}$ and its defining unitary action, retaining the unrestricted phase ambiguity only for arbitrary individual Weyl implementers. [F4, step 1.2, step 2.1, step 4.1, step 5.1, step 5.2, algebra] ∎
'''
p.write_text(s)
